  "analysis": "We need to compute the xor of squared answers over all 2^24 possible vowel subsets for a dictionary of n 3-letter words over the first 24 letters (a..x). A word is correct if it contains at least one vowel. If we denote by mask the bitset of letters present in a word (ignoring multiplicities), then for a vowel set S, the word is correct iff (mask & S) != 0. Let C be the complement of S (the consonants). The number of correct words for a given S is n - f(C), where f(C) = number of words whose mask is a subset of C. As S runs over all subsets, C also runs over all subsets, so the xor over all S equals the xor over all C of (n - f(C))^2.\n\nThus we need, for every C in [0, 2^24), f(C) = sum_{M subset of C} freq[M], where freq[M] is the count of words whose letter-set mask equals M. We can compute f(C) for all C using SOS (subset-sum) DP over 24 bits. Steps:\n- Build freq[M] by mapping each word to its bitmask M (bits for unique letters in the word).\n- Run SOS DP to transform freq into F where F[C] = sum_{M subset of C} freq[M].\n- For every C, t = n - F[C], accumulate ans ^= (t * t).\n\nThis uses 2^24 ≈ 16.8 million entries. Memory ~ 64 MB for int array, time ~ 24 * 2^24 additions (~400M), which is acceptable in C++ with optimization. Edge cases include words with repeated letters (mask remains correct) and all words with various masks. The final answer is a 64-bit value due to squaring, though values stay within reasonable bounds; we use unsigned long long for safety.",
  "plan": "Implement a C++ solution using 2^24-sized array F to store freq and then perform SOS DP to obtain F[C] = sum of freq[M] for all M ⊆ C. Finally, iterate all C to compute (n - F[C])^2 and XOR into the answer. Steps:\n- Read n and all words. For each word, compute its mask by setting bits for each distinct character, then F[mask]++.\n- For i in 0..23, for mask in 0..(1<<24)-1, if (mask & (1<<i)) F[mask] += F[mask ^ (1<<i)].\n- long long ans = 0; for mask in 0..(1<<24)-1: t = n - F[mask]; ans ^= (unsigned long long)(t) * (unsigned long long)(t).\n- Print ans.\n\nThis approach is straightforward and fits the constraints. We’ll provide a batch of shell commands that create the C++ source, compile, and run a quick sample check.",
